Let $d\ge1$, mass $m>0$ in kilograms, reference momentum $p_*>0$ in kg m s$^{-1}$, energy scale $E_0>0$ in joules, and dimensionless inverse temperature $b>0$. On $\mathbb R^d$ use $r(dp)=dp/p_*^d$ and $K(p)=|p|^2/(2mE_0)$. Then
$$\kappa_b:=\int e^{-bK(p)}r(dp)=\left( rac{2\pi mE_0}{bp_*^2}ight)^{d/2},\qquad 
u_b(dp)=\kappa_b^{-1}e^{-bK(p)}r(dp)$$
is a probability with $E_{
u_b}p_i=0$, $E_{
u_b}p_ip_j=(mE_0/b)\delta_{ij}$, and $E_{
u_b}K=d/(2b)$. For each $g$ among $K,p_i,p_ip_j$, and tilt parameter $t$ in reciprocal units to $g$, the centered logarithmic moment-generating function
$$c_g(t):=\log E_{
u_b}\exp(t(g-E_{
u_b}g))$$
is finite and twice continuously differentiable on a neighborhood of $0$, with $c_g(0)=c'_g(0)=0$. In particular $c_g(t)=O(t^2)$ as $t	o0$. Here $p_i$ has momentum units and $K$ is energy in units of $E_0$. For the optional physical-temperature parametrization, fix a positive scalar conversion parameter $k_B$ with units J K$^{-1}$ and take $T>0$ in kelvin; then $b=E_0/(k_BT)$.
